  title="Self-host"
  description="Run your own Rconn server: free, open source, one Docker container on a Linux machine with a public address."
>
  <div class="wrap page-head">
    <h1>Run your own server</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The Rconn server is free and open source. It introduces your devices to each other and
      relays traffic when they can’t connect directly. Run it yourself and your devices meet
      on a machine you control.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="need-title">
    <div class="wrap"><div class="prose">
      <p class="draft-note" role="note">
        <strong>Preview.</strong> The install script and server image aren’t published at
        these addresses yet. The commands show how setup will work and may change before
        release.
      </p>

      <h2 id="need-title">You’ll need</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>A Linux machine with a public IP address: Ubuntu, Debian, Amazon Linux or RHEL.</li>
        <li>A DNS name pointing at it, such as <code>rconn.example.com</code>.</li>
        <li>These ports open: TCP 443, UDP 3478 and UDP 49160–49260.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
      <p>On the server, run:</p>
      <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://rconn.app/install.sh \
  | sudo RCONN_HOSTNAME=rconn.example.com bash</code></pre>
      <p>
        The script installs Docker if needed, creates the server’s identity, and starts the
        server with its built-in relay. When it finishes it prints:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>the <strong>server URL</strong>, like <code>wss://rconn.example.com:443/v1/ws</code></li>
        <li>the <strong>server pin</strong>, a fingerprint starting with <code>sha256:</code></li>
        <li>an admin login, shown once</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="point">Point Rconn at it</h2>
      <p>
        In Rconn, open the <strong>Server</strong> section and enter the server URL and the
        server pin. The pin lets each device check it’s talking to your server and not an
        impostor.
      </p>

      <h2 id="compose">Prefer Docker Compose?</h2>
      <p>The installer writes a file like this to <code>/opt/rconn/compose.yaml</code>:</p>
      <pre><code>services:
  signaling:
    image: ghcr.io/rconn/signaling:latest
    network_mode: host
    restart: unless-stopped
    environment:
      RCONN_BIND: 0.0.0.0:443
      RCONN_SERVER_ID: wss://rconn.example.com:443/v1/ws
      RCONN_TURN_PUBLIC_IP: 203.0.113.10
    volumes:
      - rconn-data:/data
    command: ["serve"]
volumes:
  rconn-data:</code></pre>
      <p>
        It uses host networking because the relay needs its UDP ports on the machine’s own
        address. Before the first start, create the server’s identity, which prints the server
        pin:
      </p>
      <pre><code>docker compose run --rm signaling init --hostname rconn.example.com
docker compose up -d</code></pre>
    </div>
    </div>
